<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--
      DockableVM.ImageGeometry (NINA.WPF.Base) is a filled GeometryGroup, not a stroked icon — it
      defaults to a "PuzzlePieceSVG" resource when a plugin doesn't set one, which is exactly the
      generic jigsaw-piece look already replaced for the plugin-list icon (docs/icon.png, a
      stroked PNG).

      Redesigned 2026-09-07, real user report from a side-by-side screenshot: the original icon
      here (two concentric circles + a small orbiting dot, an annulus standing in for a stroked
      "orbit ring" glyph) read as near-identical to Orbitals' own dockable-panel tab icon at the
      small, monochrome size NINA actually renders tab icons at — unsurprising in hindsight, since
      "a ring with a dot orbiting it" is the single most generic way to draw "orbit," and Orbitals
      already owns that exact visual space by name. Rather than trying to make a subtly-different
      ring (still liable to read the same at 16-20px), this is a genuinely different silhouette —
      a comet, a filled nucleus with a curved, tapering tail — which is both more specific to
      this plugin's actual subject (comet/asteroid tracking, not orbital mechanics generally) and
      has nothing in common with a closed ring shape even at a glance. EvenOdd is harmless here
      (the tail path and the nucleus circle barely overlap, no real holes to worry about) but kept
      for consistency with how this resource was structured before.
    -->
    <GeometryGroup x:Key="PerihelionOrbitSVG" FillRule="EvenOdd">
        <!-- Nucleus -->
        <EllipseGeometry Center="16.5,7.5" RadiusX="3.2" RadiusY="3.2" />
        <!-- Tapering tail, swept down and to the left away from the nucleus -->
        <PathGeometry Figures="M 14.3,9.2 C 11,10.5 7.5,13.5 3,21 C 8,17.5 12,14.5 16.8,10.4 Z" />
    </GeometryGroup>
</ResourceDictionary>
